{"answer":"In den, a "class that behaves like a NixOS module tree" is built with the **`forward` battery**: aspects write content at `myconf`, den merges that content per host, and the merged content is evaluated as a module at a path you choose on an existing class (e.g. `nixos`). You then reach the evaluated result through that class's config, or export it as a standalone flake attribute.

## 1. Create the class with `den.batteries.forward`

```nix
myconfFwd = { class, aspect-chain }:
  den.batteries.forward {
    each       = lib.singleton true;
    fromClass  = _: "myconf";          # the class your aspects write
    intoClass  = _: "nixos";           # target class whose module system evaluates it
    intoPath   = _: [ "myconf" ];      # attribute path in that class
    fromAspect = _: lib.head aspect-chain;
  };

den.schema.host.includes = [ myconfFwd ];   # applies to every host
```

`den.batteries.forward` is defined in `modules/aspects/batteries/forward.nix` (`__functor = _: den.lib.forward.forwardEach`), and `nix/lib/forward.nix` builds the per-item module that carries `meta.__forward` into the pipe layer.

Then declare the option tree once, on the nixos side (as a host/default aspect):

```nix
den.aspects.myconf-opts.nixos = {
  options.myconf = lib.mkOption {
    type = lib.types.submodule {
      options = {
        greeting = lib.mkOption { type = lib.types.str; default = "hi"; };
        # ...your tree
      };
    };
  };
};
```

Now any number of aspects set `myconf` content:

```nix
den.aspects.a.myconf.greeting = "hello a";
den.aspects.b.myconf = { /* ... */ };
```

Den merges the class content across all aspects reachable from a host (same merge/collision semantics the output classes have: different names merge, same name with different values → `den: the option '<name>' has conflicting definitions from multiple aspects`), and the target class's module system evaluates the merged content as one module tree — **per host**. Den's own CI test does exactly this: in `templates/ci/modules/public-api/forward-from-custom-class.nix`, `test-forward-into-subpath` forwards a `src` class into the `fwd-box` submodule of `nixos` and the evaluated host config merges both sides: `expected = [ "from-nixos-owned" "from-src-class" ]`, read as `igloo.fwd-box.items`. `test-forward-custom-class-to-nixos` shows the root-path variant (`igloo.networking.hostName`).

Optional convenience: register the class so the forward only names the source:

```nix
den.classes.myconf = {
  description = "my module tree";
  forwardTo = { class = "nixos"; path = [ "myconf" ]; };
};
```

(see `docs/.../guides/custom-classes.mdx`; the lower-level equivalent is `den.classes.<name>` + a `den.lib.policy.route` policy.)

## 2. Putting the result in a flake output

**If routed into `nixos`, it's already in a flake output**: `flake.nixosConfigurations.<host>.config.myconf` (part of the host's toplevel). Often that's enough.

**Standalone top-level attribute** (`flake.myconf.<host>`): the `flake` class supports a parametric `{ host }` form that gives one definition per host (`guides/flake-outputs.mdx`, discussion #181). Declare the option for merge semantics, then:

```nix
# top-level module that owns the flake:
options.flake.myconf = lib.mkOption {
  default = { };
  type = lib.types.lazyAttrsOf lib.types.raw;
};

# one entry per host:
den.aspects.export-myconf.flake =
  { host, config, ... }:
  {
    myconf.${host.name} =
      config.flake.nixosConfigurations.${host.name}.config.myconf;
  };

den.schema.host.includes = [ den.aspects.export-myconf ];
```

Caution from the guide: the `flake` class merges only **one level deep**, so keep the per-host key at the first level (as above) — the evaluated tree as the value merges fine. Don't hang this off `den.schema.flake.includes`; per-host content belongs to the host scope.

**Evaluate the class module yourself and export it as a package/app** (useful when `myconf` doesn't live inside an OS class) — this is the `nvf-standalone` template pattern (`templates/nvf-standalone/modules/`):

```nix
den.aspects.flake.packages =
  { pkgs, ... }:
  let
    aspect = den.lib.parametric.fixedTo ctx {
      includes = [ myconfFwd den.aspects.my-config ];
    };
    module   = den.lib.aspects.resolve "myconf" aspect;   # merged module
    evaluated = (lib.evalModules { modules = [ myconfOpts module ]; }).config;
  in
  {
    myconf-pkg = pkgs.writeText "myconf" (builtins.toJSON evaluated);
  };

den.schema.flake-system.includes = [ den.aspects.flake ];
```

`den.lib.aspects.resolve <class> <resolved-aspect>` (`nix/lib/aspects/default.nix`, `resolve = fxResolveTree`) returns the merged class module; the nvf template feeds that to its module-system entry point and lands the result at `flake.packages.<system>.my-neovim` (`nix run .#my-neovim`), importing `inputs.den.flakeOutputs.packages` to declare the `flake.packages` option when flake-parts is absent.

## How I know

- `denful/den@main` (fetched raw; I don't have a pinned rev): `nix/lib/forward.nix` (forward mechanics, `meta.__forward`), `modules/aspects/batteries/forward.nix` (`den.batteries.forward`), `nix/lib/aspects/default.nix` (`resolve = fxResolveTree`), `templates/ci/modules/public-api/forward-from-custom-class.nix` (merge-into-submodule test), `templates/nvf-standalone/modules/{den,nvf-integration}.nix` (resolve → flake package), `nix/flakeOutputs.nix` (merge-aware `flake.*` option declarations).
- Official guides `Custom Nix Classes` and `Flake Outputs from Aspects` (den.denful.dev).

One honest caveat: the `eval` and `clone` tools were unavailable in this environment, so nothing was executed — I verified by reading source and the project's own tests, not by running `nix flake check`.","confidence":0.75,"gaps":["eval tool refused in this environment ('eval is unavailable in this evaluation'); no attribute was actually evaluated, all evidence is raw source reads of denful/den@main without a pinned rev","pipe-layer code that consumes meta.__forward (where the merged class content is evaluated as a module at intoPath) was not read; the per-host evaluation claim rests on the CI test expectations in forward-from-custom-class.nix","module arguments available inside a flake-class module (config.flake.nixosConfigurations...) come from the docs examples, not a source read","den.provides.forward (used in the CI tests) vs den.batteries.forward (used in docs/batteries) aliasing not confirmed in source"],"escalate_forced":"no"}

