function.arguments:
{"answer":"**Short answer: no hand-rolled aggregation over `den.aspects` — and you only need a *thin* aggregating aspect if your class has to land inside a module system.** Den's class-resolution pipeline already collects a class's content from every aspect in an entity's tree; the only thing you add is the routing point, which den provides as a first-class battery.

How it works, from the source (`denful/den`, `main`):

1. **Aggregation is the pipeline's job, not yours.** `den.lib.aspects.resolve <class> (den.lib.resolveEntity <entity> ctx)` builds the entity's whole aspect tree (`nix/lib/resolve-entity.nix`: `includes = selfProvide ++ schemaIncludes ++ instanceCollection "includes"`) and the fx pipeline merges every contributing aspect's `<class>` content per entity. So a custom class defined on any number of aspects is already aggregated for each host/user/home — you never need `lib.attrValues den.aspects` + manual picks, and hand-rolling that loop would bypass `includes`/`excludes` and the schema/instance tiering.

2. **If the class must land inside a Nix module system** (e.g. `users.users.<name>`, an OCI container, a nixos path): register the class (`den.classes.<name> = { description = ...; forwardTo ? ...; }`) and include the forward aspect the battery returns into the schema — *that* is the "dedicated aggregating aspect", and it's declarative, not a collector you write:
   ```nix
   den.batteries.forward {
     each = lib.attrValues host.users;
     fromClass = _: "container";
     intoClass = _: host.class;
     intoPath  = _: [ "virtualisation" "oci-containers" "containers" ];
     fromAspect = _: user.aspect;
   }
   ```
   included via `den.schema.user.includes = [ fwd ]` (guide: `docs/src/content/docs/guides/custom-classes.mdx`). Its `forwardItem`s read `item.resolved` — the merged aspect (`nix/lib/forward.nix`) — so "collecting from all aspects" happens inside resolution. This is exactly how den implements its own `user`, `homeManager`, `hjem`, and `maid` classes, and the battery's own docstring says it "returns an aspect that needs to be included for the new class to exist."

3. **If you only want the collected class at the flake level** (e.g. a per-class output, no module system): resolve it directly, the way den does for its own `flake`/`flake-parts` classes in `modules/outputs.nix`:
   ```nix
   den.lib.aspects.resolve "files" (den.lib.resolveEntity "flake-parts" { })
   ```
   The docs' lower-level example (`den.classes.files` + `den.lib.policy.route` in `den.schema.flake-parts.includes`) is the policy-form of the same thing.

So: one small forward/route aspect included in the schema (or a bare `resolve` for flake-level output) — not a manual walk of `den.aspects`.","confidence":0.8,"gaps":["Source read at denful/den@main via web_fetch (clone unavailable in this evaluation); not evaluated against the asker's specific repo/rev, so version drift is unverified","The fx pipeline internals (nix/lib/aspects/fx/) were not read in detail; the 'resolve merges across all aspects' claim rests on resolve-entity.nix structure, forward.nix reading item.resolved, and the docs guide","Asker's exact use case (which class, routing into a module system vs flake-level output) was not stated, so the answer covers both routes"],"escalate_forced":"no"}

