{"answer":"Short answer: **a policy, not an aspect.** In den, aspects configure an entity that already exists; the host↔user association is *topology*, and topology is the policy layer's job (den's README principle: "Policy — how entities relate"). The built-in association is `den.hosts.<sys>.<host>.users.<name>` fanned out by `den.policies.host-to-users`, so there are two ways to let a profile decide the set:

## Option 1 — the minimal version (no new machinery)

`users` is a plain option on the host (`nix/lib/entities/host.nix`: `users = ... attrsOf (userType config)`), so a profile can just be a shared attrset, referenced in one line per host:

```nix
# profiles.nix
userProfiles = {
  office = {
    alice = { classes = [ "homeManager" ]; };
    bob   = { classes = [ "homeManager" ]; };
  };
  server = { svc = { classes = [ "homeManager" ]; }; };
};
```

```nix
# hosts
den.hosts.x86_64-linux.igloo.users  = userProfiles.office;
den.hosts.x86_64-linux.server.users = userProfiles.server;
```

The set is defined once in the profile; hosts only name the profile. Everything downstream works as-is because the records go through the full user module evaluation (defaults, `id_hash`, aspect lookup `den.aspects.<user>` / `den.aspects."<user>@<host>"`).

## Option 2 — a profile as a policy (fully decoupled)

If you don't even want the `users = ...` line, mirror `den.policies.host-to-users` (`modules/policies/core.nix`) and fan out to the profile's users:

```nix
den.policies.office =
  { host, ... }:
  lib.concatMap (
    user: [ (den.lib.policy.resolve.shared { user = user // { inherit host; } }) ]
  ) (lib.attrValues userProfiles.office);
```

Attach it per host via the instance `includes` key (read by `nix/lib/resolve-entity.nix`, which merges `entity.includes` after the schema tier):

```nix
den.hosts.x86_64-linux.igloo = {
  includes = [ den.policies.office ];
};
```

For one policy serving many profiles, read a freeform attribute: `den.schema.host.includes = [ den.policies.office-or-server ]` and branch inside on `host.profile`.

Two things to get right:

- **User records must carry what the pipeline reads.** Declared users get `name`, `userName`, `classes`, `aspect` (defaulted to `den.aspects.<name>`) from the `userType` module; a raw profile record does not. Set at least `name`, `userName`, `classes` (note: the default is `[ "user" ]`, so you must opt into `[ "homeManager" ]` explicitly, e.g. via `den.schema.user.classes = lib.mkDefault [ "homeManager" ]`) and `aspect = den.aspects.alice`.
- **Delivery is automatic once the user scope exists.** The home-manager battery (`nix/lib/home-env.nix`) attaches an `hm-user-detect` policy to every user scope (`den.schema.user.includes`, see `modules/aspects/batteries/home-manager.nix`), and the docs (`guides/home-manager.mdx`) state it explicitly "covers users resolved via the registry or other policies, rather than declared directly on `host.users`". It forwards each such user's `homeManager` class into `home-manager.users.<userName>` and imports the HM module — so policy-fanned users land in the host config with no extra wiring.

My recommendation: start with Option 1 — one shared definition, one line per host — and only move to Option 2 if you need the user set to be computed (e.g. from fleet data) rather than referenced.

