function.arguments:
{"answer":"In den, host → all-users Home Manager configuration is delivered from the **host's aspect** via the special `provides.to-users` key:

```nix
# myhost is a declared host: den.hosts.x86_64-linux.myhost = { ... }
den.aspects.myhost = {
  provides.to-users = { user, ... }: {
    homeManager = {
      home.stateVersion = "25.11";
      programs.git.enable = true;
    };
  };
};
```

- `provides.to-users` delivers to **every user on that host**. `provides` registered on a host aspect is subtree-scoped to that host, so users on other hosts are not affected — exactly "one specific host".
- Cross-entity routing is built into the pipeline: no battery needs to be enabled (the old `mutual-provider` battery is an inert compatibility shim).
- The function gets the `user` context, so per-user variation is possible: `home.packages = lib.optionals (user.name == "alice") [ pkgs.tmux ];`

**Prerequisite — Home Manager must be enabled for the users.** Den only forwards `homeManager` content to `home-manager.users.<name>` for users that have the class:

```nix
# per user:
den.hosts.x86_64-linux.myhost.users.alice.classes = [ "homeManager" ];

# or as the default for all users, unless they override:
den.schema.user.classes = lib.mkDefault [ "homeManager" ];
```

Without `homeManager` in a user's `classes`, the content has no delivery target and silently never reaches that user.

**Variants**
- One specific user on that host: named provide — `den.aspects.myhost.provides.alice.homeManager.programs.vim.enable = true;`
- Per-user named sub-aspects: `den.aspects.myhost.alice.homeManager = { ... };` (entity-named freeform keys on the host aspect are auto-included into that user's scope by the `host-to-users` policy).
- `den.batteries.host-aspects` forwards the host aspect's `homeManager` key to users that opt in via `den.aspects.<user>.includes = [ den.batteries.host-aspects ]` — workable, but each user must include it, so `provides.to-users` is the simpler route for "all users".

**Pitfall:** older den releases let a host-scope `{ host, user }` parametric aspect *leak* its `homeManager` content to the users. That leak was removed (upstream issue #609, fixed in main @ 2589d73): a host-scope `{ user, ... }` aspect now fans out but emits class-locally on the host, where `homeManager` is inert — so that form no longer reaches the users. If you have config written in that style, move it to `provides.to-users`.

**How I know:** read in `github.com/sini/den` (main @ `2589d73`, 2026-06-15): `docs/src/content/docs/guides/mutual.mdx` ("provides.to-users delivers to every user on the host"; "Configuring peers from the host"), `docs/src/content/docs/guides/home-manager.mdx` (Enabling Homes: per-user / `den.schema.user.classes` default; "Host-scope parametric aspects no longer deliver homeManager content to users"), `modules/policies/core.nix` (host-to-users policy), `modules/aspects/batteries/host-aspects.nix` (opt-in battery), `nix/lib/entities/host.nix` (users declared per host with `classes` default `[ "user" ]`), and the regression test `templates/ci/modules/deadbugs/hasaspect-host-provides-to-users.nix`, which asserts a host-aspect `provides.to-users` lands in `igloo.home-manager.users.tux`.","confidence":0.85,"gaps":["eval and clone were unavailable in this environment: I verified the to-users delivery path from docs, source and the repo's regression tests, but did not run the denTest suite myself (CI 1023/1023 is claimed in the 2589d73 commit message, not verified here)","I did not read the exact code site where provides.to-users is translated into cross-entity policy effects (documented in mutual.mdx as automatic); I verified the end-to-end behavior via the hasaspect-host-provides-to-users test expectations instead"],"escalate_forced":"no"}

