     {
       options.includes = lib.mkOption {
         description = "Aspects and policies included for this instance only.";
         type = lib.types.listOf lib.types.raw;
         default = [ ];
       };
       options.excludes = ...;
     }
   ];
   ```
   and `config.den.schema.host.inherits = [ den.schema.conf ]` — so `host` (and `user`, and `home`) all accept `includes` on their entries. The comment there explains the intent: an instance's own `includes`/`excludes` are read by `resolveEntity` for every kind, and they had to be declared explicitly because a strict instance type otherwise *refused* the key.

2. **It's actually consumed.** In `nix/lib/resolve-entity.nix`:
   ```nix
   instanceCollection = key: if entity == null || !builtins.isAttrs entity then [ ] else entity.${key} or [ ];
   ...
   includes = selfProvide ++ schemaIncludes ++ instanceCollection "includes";
   ```
   The comment there names your exact path: *"The instance's own collections (`den.hosts.<sys>.<n>.includes`, and the same key on any other entity kind)"*. So the key is honored on every entity kind that declares `isEntity`, which hosts do.

