In den you normally don't reach for `specialArgs` at all — and when you do, the knob is the host's `instantiate` option.

**1. Prefer the den context (no specialArgs needed).**
Class module functions get den's context args injected automatically — that's the whole point of den. Write:

```nix
den.aspects.my-feature.nixos = { host, user, config, pkgs, ... }: {
  networking.hostName = host.name;
};
```

Den's pipeline wraps your function and merges the context values (`host`, `user`, `home`, `den`, …) in; `config`/`pkgs`/`...` come from the module system as usual (see `wrapFunctionModule` in `nix/lib/aspects/fx/class-module.nix` — den args are pulled from the pipeline `ctx`, remaining args from the module system). So if the value you want is data about an entity (a host, a user, some quirk), put it on the entity record (e.g. `den.hosts.<system>.<name>.<yourKey>`) and read it via `host.<yourKey>` — no wrapper, no specialArgs, no recursion, which is exactly what the den docs promise ("no wrappers, no specialArgs, no infinite recursion").

**2. For values that genuinely need `specialArgs` (like `inputs`): override the host's `instantiate`.**

`den.hosts.<system>.<name>` carries an `instantiate` option (defined in `nix/lib/entities/host.nix`). Its own description says:

> "Function used to instantiate the OS configuration. Depending on class, defaults to … `nixos`: `inputs.nixpkgs.lib.nixosSystem`. Set explicitly if you need: … **adding specialArgs when absolutely required**."

It is called with the assembled module bundle — `{ modules; pkgs; }` (see `mkInstantiateArgs` in `nix/lib/aspects/fx/resolve.nix`, which builds `{ modules; pkgs?; }` and `applyInstantiates` calls `spec.instantiate` on it). So:

```nix
den.hosts.x86_64-linux.igloo = {
  instantiate =
    { modules, pkgs }:
    pkgs.nixpkgs.lib.nixosSystem {
      inherit modules;
      specialArgs = { inherit inputs; };
    };
};
```

(`inputs` is already a module arg of your den module tree — den's top-level eval passes `specialArgs.inputs = inputs`, see `modules/outputs.nix` — so the closure can just capture it.)

