{"answer":"On modern Nix (2.21+, including the current 2.34):

- `builtins.functionArgs ({ a, b ? 1, ... }: a)` → `{ a = false; b = true; }`
- `builtins.functionArgs (x: x)` → `{ }` (an empty set)

`functionArgs` returns a set mapping each **named** formal argument to a **Boolean** saying whether that argument has a default value (`true` if it has a `?` default, `false` if it's required). It does not return the default values themselves, and it does **not** include `...`: the ellipsis is a flag on the lambda, not an attribute, so it never appears in the result. A plain positional lambda has no formals at all, hence the empty set.

How I know: in `src/libexpr/primops.cc` on `nixos/nix` (master), `prim_functionArgs` does:

```cpp
if (const auto & formals = args[0]->lambda().fun->getFormals()) {
    auto attrs = state.buildBindings(formals->formals.size());
    for (auto & i : formals->formals)
        attrs.insert(i.name, state.getBool(i.def), i.pos);
    ...
} else {
    v.mkAttrs(&Bindings::emptyBindings);
    return;
}
```

i.e. one attribute per named formal, value = `true` iff a default expression exists; empty set when `getFormals()` is null (positional lambda). The same file's doc comment and the 2.34.9 manual both state exactly this: `functionArgs ({ x, y ? 123}: ...) = { x = false; y = true; }` and `functionArgs (x: ...) = { }`.

Version note: on older Nix (pre-2.21, e.g. 2.19 and earlier) the semantics were different — it returned a copy of the argument *pattern*, so you'd have seen `{ a = null; b = 1; ... = ...; }` for the first function and `{ ... = ...; }` for `x: x`. If you're on an old Nix, that's what to expect.
