In your NixOS configuration, set `PermitRootLogin` under `services.openssh.settings`:

```nix
services.openssh = {
  enable = true;
  settings.PermitRootLogin = "no";
};
```

then `nixos-rebuild switch`. `"no"` forbids all root SSH logins (password *and* key-based).

How I know:
- In nixpkgs master, the OpenSSH module (`nixos/modules/services/networking/ssh/sshd.nix`) declares `PermitRootLogin` as a typed option of `services.openssh.settings`, with `default = "prohibit-password"` (i.e. by default root *can* log in with a key) and allowed values `yes`, `without-password`, `prohibit-password`, `forced-commands-only`, `no`. `settings` is passed verbatim into the generated `/etc/ssh/sshd_config`, so `"no"` takes effect there.
- The official NixOS wiki SSH page shows the same `PermitRootLogin = "no"` inside a hardened server setup.

Two things to watch:
- The old `services.openssh.permitRootLogin` (lowercase) is a renamed alias for the `settings.PermitRootLogin` form — use the `settings` spelling in new configs.
- If you also use `services.openssh.extraConfig`, don't repeat `PermitRootLogin` there: the module has an assertion that fails the build on duplicate sshd_config keys ("Duplicate sshd config key; does your capitalization match the option's?").

Tip: if you want to keep root reachable only for emergencies, `forced-commands-only` (or `without-password` with `PasswordAuthentication = false`) lets you test that a non-root user with sudo works before you lock the door.

