{"answer":"Yes — den gives you all three pieces: a custom class whose content is a real NixOS-style module tree, merging across aspects, and per-host placement into flake outputs. Everything below is from `denful/den` at `main` (I checked the source, not just docs).

**1. Register the class** — one line (option defined in `modules/options.nix`; each `den.classes.<name>` entry is `{ description, forwardTo, parentPath, parentArg }`). Leave `forwardTo`/`parentPath`/`parentArg` at their `null` defaults: that marks `myconf` as a root class that owns its own top-level config, like `nixos`.

```nix
{ den, ... }: {
  den.classes.myconf = { description = "My custom module tree"; };
}
```

**2. Aspects set content; the pieces merge like modules.** Each aspect writes an ordinary module under the `myconf` key; den collects `myconf` from every aspect reachable from the scope and merges it with module-system semantics (the same treatment `nixos`/`darwin` get):

```nix
den.aspects.networking = {
  myconf = { pkgs, ... }: { myopt.foo = "bar"; myopt.list = [ 1 ]; };
};

den.aspects.host1 = {
  myconf = { pkgs, ... }: { myopt.list = [ 2 ]; }; # merges, no collision
  includes = [ den.aspects.networking ];
};
```

You supply the main module (your analog of nixpkgs' `top-level.nix`) declaring the options of the tree. Two caveats from the docs: an aspect in `den.aspects` contributes nothing until something includes it, and the merged *flake* class only merges one level deep, so keep per-host keys at the first level.

**3. Evaluating the class.** Den resolves a class's content with `den.lib.aspects.resolve "myconf" <aspect>` (`nix/lib/aspects/default.nix`); that returns the merged module tree, which you feed to `lib.evalModules` together with your main module:

```nix
evalMyconf =
  host:
  (lib.evalModules {
    modules = [ myconfMain (den.lib.aspects.resolve "myconf" den.aspects.${host.name}) ];
    specialArgs = { pkgs = inputs.nixpkgs.legacyPackages.${host.system}; };
  }).config;
```

This is exactly what den's own `templates/nvf-standalone` does with its custom `vim`/`nvf` classes: resolve the class, hand the module to a module system, expose a result as a flake output.

**4. Putting the result in the flake — three working routes:**

- **Top-level, per host (recommended for your case).** Write to the `flake` class in parametric `{ host }` form — the flake class is a genuine `lib.evalModules` tree (`modules/outputs.nix` evaluates `den.lib.aspects.resolve "flake" (den.lib.resolveEntity "flake" {})` with `inputs.den.flakeOutputs.flake` into `.config.flake`), and the parametric form yields one definition per host:

```nix
{ den, lib, ... }: {
  den.aspects.myconf-out.flake =
    { host }:
    {
      flake.myConf.${host.name} = evalMyconf host;
    };
  den.schema.host.includes = [ den.aspects.myconf-out ];

  # declare the target so contributions merge (needed when flake-parts is absent):
  options.flake.myConf = lib.mkOption {
    type = lib.types.lazyAttrsOf lib.types.raw;
    default = { };
  };
}
```

  Without flake-parts nothing declares `flake.myConf`, so undeclared attributes can fail to merge (the #317 warning in `nix/flakeOutputs.nix`); declaring it via `den.schema.flake` is the documented place for your own attributes.
- **Per-system outputs** (`packages`, `legacyPackages`, …): write them from a flake-system aspect, `den.schema.flake-system.includes = [ den.aspects.flake ]` — the nvf-standalone pattern; use `legacyPackages` for nested `images.<host>`-style layouts.
- **The den-native per-host mechanism:** host entities already carry `instantiate`, `intoAttr` and `mainModule` (`nix/lib/entities/host.nix`) — `instantiate` defaults to `nixosSystem`/`darwinSystem`/`systemManager.makeSystemConfig`, `intoAttr` defaults to `["nixosConfigurations" <name>]`, and `mainModule` is `{ imports = <resolved class content> }`. `den.policies.system-to-os-outputs` (`modules/policies/flake.nix`) emits `den.lib.policy.instantiate host` per host — that's what turns a host's class into `nixosConfigurations.<name>`. Since `instantiate`/`intoAttr` are overridable host options, the fully-pipeline version of your setup is: set the host's `instantiate` to a function that runs your `myconf` `evalModules` and `intoAttr = [ "myConf" <host name> ]`, and the pipeline places it for you.

Note: in the output classes, `pkgs` is `legacyPackages.<system>` with no overlays; for your custom class you control `pkgs` yourself via `specialArgs`, as above.","confidence":0.72,"gaps":["Did not read nfx/nix-effects' instantiate-effect materializer, so the exact argument shape host.instantiate receives at materialization time is inferred from the option docs (nix/lib/entities/host.nix), not verified","No eval run: no user repo/rev was provided, and the den repo's own flake check was not evaluated to confirm the recipes end-to-end","Cross-aspect merging of a custom (non-OS) class bucket is inferred from the nixos path and the docs' collisions table; the fx bucket-collection code (nix/lib/aspects/fx) was not read line-by-line"],"escalate_forced":"no"}

