{"answer":"**Short answer:** in den, the *set* of users on a host is host data (`den.hosts.<system>.<host>.users.<name>`), and the built-in `host-to-users` policy (`modules/policies/core.nix`) fans out over exactly that. No built-in merge lets a profile aspect write into `host.users` — so a profile can't literally add `users.<name>` entries, but it can own everything else, and a small policy can make the profile's user list the topology.

**What a profile aspect can already decide (no new machinery):**
- **User content lives in the user aspect, not the host.** The `user` class on `den.aspects.<user>` is routed to the host's `users.users.<name>` by the built-in `user-to-host` policy (`modules/aspects/batteries/os-user.nix`), active by default via `den.default.includes`. `define-user`/`primary-user`/`user-shell` batteries do the same for accounts; `homeManager`/`hjem`/`maid` classes are forwarded to `home-manager.users.<name>` by the home batteries (`nix/lib/home-env.nix`).
- **Host/profile → user delivery:** `provides.to-users` / `provides.<user>` on a host aspect (Mutual Providers), the `den.batteries.host-aspects` battery (user opts in via `den.aspects.<user>.includes`), or a host-scope aspect destructuring `{ user, ... }` which fans out over the host's users (verified by `templates/ci/modules/features/relationship-fanout.nix`).

**If the profile should own the user *set* — write a policy.** `den.lib.policy.resolve.to "user"` creates user scopes (the Policies doc shows exactly this for `host-to-users`; `core.nix` uses the shared variant), so:

```nix
den.aspects.workstation = {
  users = { alice = { }; bob = { }; };   # pure data on the profile
  nixos = { /* … */ };
  includes = [ den.policies.workstation-users ];
};

den.policies.workstation-users = { host, ... }:
  let inherit (den.lib.policy) resolve;
  in
  lib.map (u: (resolve.to "user" { user = u; }))
    (builtins.attrValues den.aspects.workstation.users);
```

Policies activate by appearing in `includes` lists, including an aspect's own subtree (Policy Activation docs), so putting it in the profile's `includes` scopes it to exactly the hosts using that profile. Policy-resolved users are first-class for the wiring: `user-to-host` fires on any user scope with a host in context, and the home batteries' `*-user-detect` policy explicitly "covers users resolved by registry/policy rather than declared directly on `host.users`" (comment in `nix/lib/home-env.nix`).

**Pragmatic fallback:** keep `users.<name> = {}` as a one-line topology entry per host (no content) and put all user config in `den.aspects.<user>` plus profile-side `provides.to-users` — that's the idiomatic den split (host owns topology, user owns content).

**Caveat:** I verified all of this against `denful/den@main` source and docs, but I didn't evaluate your repo (none provided), and the exact `user` binding shape for a policy-resolved scope is an internal detail (instances normally come pre-formed from `host.users`; the `den.aspects."<user>@<host>"`/`den.aspects.<user>` auto-lookup rides on the instance type in `host.nix`). Share your repo + den pin and I'll eval the recipe against your setup before you adopt it.","confidence":0.7,"gaps":["Did not eval the asker's repo (no repo/rev given): the policy-resolved-user recipe is untested against their pinned den version","Did not verify that a plain attrset user binding in resolve.to "user" auto-resolves den.aspects.<user> (aspect lookup rides on the user instance type built from den.schema.user in nix/lib/entities/host.nix; the home-env battery passes instances formed from host.users)","Read den@main (692 commits) via web; the asker's den pin may differ (policy/provides API is in active rework)"],"escalate_forced":"no"}

