{"answer":"It goes in a **host-qualified aspect name**: `den.aspects."tux@igloo"`. That's den's built-in way of scoping one user's config to one host — no routing, no `mkIf host.name == "igloo"`.

**Setup** (verified against `denful/den` main):

```nix
# declare the user on the host (hosts.nix style, as in templates/default)
den.hosts.x86_64-linux.igloo.users.tux = {
  classes = [ "homeManager" ]; # if you want home-manager content
};

# shared tux config — applies to tux on every host he exists on
den.aspects.tux.homeManager = { pkgs, ... }: {
  home.packages = [ pkgs.htop ];
};

# ONLY on igloo — host-qualified aspect name
den.aspects."tux@igloo".homeManager = { pkgs, ... }: {
  programs.git.userEmail = "tux@igloo.example";
};
```

Why this is the right place, from the source:

- `nix/lib/entities/host.nix`, `userType`: a host-declared user's `aspect` option defaults to `lookupAspectBy den [ "${config.name}@${host.name}" config.name ]` — the comment says "Host-qualified first, so one user can be configured per host without the bare aspect having to branch on `host.name`".
- `nix/lib/entities/_types.nix`, `lookupAspectBy`: an entity takes **every** candidate aspect that exists, composed through `includes` — "NOT 'first match wins'". So `den.aspects."tux@igloo"` *adds to* `den.aspects.tux` rather than replacing it, and wins where they conflict.
