{"answer":"Because `builtins.seq` only forces its first argument to **weak head normal form (WHNF)**, and the WHNF of an attribute set is the set itself — its attribute *values* are not part of the WHNF.

Concretely, in Nix's source (`src/libexpr/primops.cc`, `NixOS/nix` master) the implementation is:

```cpp
/* Evaluate the first argument, then return the second argument. */
static void prim_seq(EvalState & state, CallSite callSite, Value * const * args, Value & v)
{
    state.forceValue(*args[0], noPos);
    state.forceValue(*args[1], noPos);
    v = *args[1];
}
```

`state.forceValue` reduces the thunk for `{ a = throw "x"; }` to a set with one binding whose value is a *thunk* for `throw "x"`. Constructing the set only requires evaluating the attribute **names** (here just the literal `a`, so nothing throws); the value expressions are left unevaluated. So `seq` finds the set "evaluated enough", returns the second argument, and `1` is the result with no exception.

The manual documents exactly this laziness at the `tryEval` entry (https://nixos.org/manual/nix/stable/language/builtins.html):

> "this doesn't evaluate *e* deeply, so `let e = { x = throw ""; }; in (builtins.tryEval e).success` is `true`. Using `builtins.deepSeq` one can get the expected result: `let e = { x = throw ""; }; in (builtins.tryEval (builtins.deepSeq e e)).success` is `false`."

Practical consequences:
- `builtins.seq { a = throw "x"; } 1` → `1` (no throw).
- `builtins.deepSeq { a = throw "x"; } 1` → **throws "x"**, because `prim_deepSeq` calls `forceValueDeep`, which recurses into set attributes and list elements (`src/libexpr/eval.cc`).
- Likewise `builtins.attrNames`, `?`/`hasAttr` won't throw on that set, while anything that reads the value — `builtins.getAttr "a"`, `.a`, `builtins.attrValues`, `mapAttrs` + use of the result — will.
- Nuance: if the *name* threw, `seq` would throw, e.g. `builtins.seq { "${throw "x"}" = 1; } 1`, since names must be evaluated to construct the set.

If you were using `seq` as a "has this been computed / does this succeed" check, that's why it doesn't work for sets with throwing values — use `builtins.deepSeq` (or `tryEval (builtins.deepSeq e e)`) to force the whole structure.","confidence":0.9,"gaps":["No live eval was run to reproduce the expression (no flake exposing it was given); verified against the Nix 2.34.9 manual and NixOS/nix master source (primops.cc prim_seq/prim_deepSeq, eval.cc forceValueDeep) instead","clone was unavailable in this environment, so source was read via raw fetches of NixOS/nix master"],"escalate_forced":"no"}

