{"answer":"No — `{ a = 1; } ? a.b` evaluates to `false`, it does not throw.

Checked against the Nix source (`NixOS/nix`, `master`, `src/libexpr/eval.cc`):

- The `?` operator is implemented by `ExprOpHasAttr::eval`, which walks the attribute path one step at a time:
  ```cpp
  for (auto & i : attrPath) {
      state.forceValue(*vAttrs, getPos());
      const Attr * j;
      auto name = getName(i, state, env);
      if (vAttrs->type() == nAttrs && (j = vAttrs->attrs()->get(name))) {
          vAttrs = j->value;
      } else {
          v.mkBool(false);
          return;
      }
  }
  v.mkBool(true);
  ```
  For `{ a = 1; } ? a.b`: step `a` succeeds (gives the int `1`); step `b` finds the current value is **not** an attrset, the `type() == nAttrs && ...` guard fails, and it returns `false`. There is no throw for the "not an attrset" case.

Contrasts:

- `.` (attribute selection) is strict: `ExprSelect::eval` calls `forceAttrs` at each path step, so `{ a = 1; } . a.b` throws a type error (value not an attribute set), not just "attribute missing".
