function.arguments:
{"answer":"Use **specialisations** — the NixOS mechanism for building variants of the same host that each appear as their own boot-menu entry. Verified against nixpkgs master source (see below).

**1. Define the variant in your host config**

```nix
{
  # base: e.g. the proprietary driver
  hardware.nvidia.enable = true;

  system.specialisation.nvidia-off = {
    # inheritParentConfig defaults to true: the variant is the whole
    # system config with these overrides layered on top
    configuration = {
      hardware.nvidia.enable = false;  # drops the whole nvidia module stack
      hardware.graphics.enable = true; # falls back to modesetting/llvmpipe
    };
  };
}
```

`configuration` is a regular NixOS module environment extended over the parent (so `lib.mkForce`, imports and any normal option work). Names must not contain `/` (assertion); nested specialisations are ignored.

**2. Build and pick at boot**

- `nixos-rebuild switch` (or `boot`) builds the base *and* every specialisation — each is a full NixOS toplevel — and the boot loader writes an extra entry titled **`NixOS (nvidia-off)`** (title comes from the `specialisation.<name>` attribute name; there is no separate display-name option).
- At boot, pick `NixOS (nvidia-off)` in the systemd-boot/GRUB menu to get the same host with the driver swapped.
- `nixos-rebuild switch --specialisation nvidia-off` (i.e. `-c nvidia-off`) makes the variant the *default* boot entry; a plain `nixos-rebuild switch` restores the base as default.
- Runtime switch without reboot: `sudo /run/current-system/specialisation/nvidia-off/bin/switch-to-configuration test` (command from the option docs; use `switch` for a full switch). A real driver change will usually still need a reboot.

**3. Caveats**

- Each specialisation costs a full extra system build per rebuild.
- Specialisation entries inherit the parent generation's sort-key, so they sit right next to their generation in the menu.
- If the variant is a substantially different system rather than a few option overrides, the heavier alternative is a separate profile: `nixos-rebuild -p <name> switch` (profile under `/nix/var/nix/profiles/system-profiles/<name>`) — each profile gets its own boot entries (title includes `[profile]`). For "same host, different driver," specialisation is the right tool.

**What I checked**

- nixpkgs master `nixos/modules/system/activation/specialisation.nix` — the `specialisation` option (`inheritParentConfig` default true, `configuration`), each child built as a toplevel linked under `$out/specialisation/<name>`, the runtime switch command, no-`/` name assertion.
- nixpkgs master `nixos/modules/system/boot/loader/systemd-boot/systemd-boot-builder.py` — reads `org.nixos.specialisation.v1` from each generation's `boot.json` and writes one loader entry per specialisation, title `NixOS (<name>)`, description `Generation N <label>, built on <date>`.
- RFC 0125 (bootspec) in NixOS/rfcs — specialisations are "multiple variants of a NixOS configuration with a single parent" in `org.nixos.specialisation.v1`; its feature matrix lists specialisation support for both systemd-boot and GRUB.
- nixos-rebuild-ng `src/nixos_rebuild/__init__.py` — the `--specialisation` / `-c` flag, "Activates given specialisation".","confidence":0.85,"gaps":["GRUB backend source not read directly (specialisation support for GRUB is attested only by RFC 0125's feature matrix)","No eval run: no repo/rev supplied from the asker, and the question is a how-to; den-specific conventions not checked (question did not name den)"],"escalate_forced":"no"}

{"answer":"The built-in way is the `virtualisation.vmVariant` option: put your VM-only options inside it.

```nix
{
  # Only applied when building/running the VM (`nixos-rebuild build-vm`)
  virtualisation.vmVariant = {
    virtualisation.memorySize = 4096;
    virtualisation.graphics = false;
    services.openssh.enable = true;
    networking.firewall.enable = false;
    # ... any other NixOS option, VM-only
  };
}
```

Why this works (from nixpkgs source, `nixos/modules/virtualisation/build-vm.nix`, identical on 23.11/24.11/25.05/master):

- `virtualisation.vmVariant` is an extended-module (default `{}`) whose description is literally: *"Machine configuration to be added for the vm script produced by `nixos-rebuild build-vm`"*.
- `system.build.vm` — the attribute `nixos-rebuild build-vm` builds — is defined as
  `system.build.vm = lib.mkDefault config.virtualisation.vmVariant.system.build.vm;`
  i.e. it's the `system.build.vm` of a *re-evaluation of your whole config* extended with the `virtualisation/qemu-vm.nix` module plus whatever you set in `vmVariant`. Your normal `system.build.toplevel` (what `switch`/`boot` use) is untouched, so these options never leak into the real system.
- `qemu-vm.nix` defines `system.build.vm` as a `run-<system-name>-vm` script (a `runCommand "nixos-vm"` derivation) that boots the config in QEMU.

Notes:

- For `nixos-rebuild build-vm-with-bootloader` there is the sibling option `virtualisation.vmVariantWithBootLoader` (same mechanism, adds `virtualisation.useBootLoader = true`).
- nixpkgs already auto-adjusts a set of options for the VM via `mkVMOverride` inside `qemu-vm.nix` (swap and LUKS disabled, host `fileSystems` replaced by virtiofs/9p mounts, `modesetting` video driver, wireless disabled, `timesyncd` off, ...). If you want to re-enable one of those for the VM only, override it inside `virtualisation.vmVariant` with `lib.mkForce`/`lib.mkOverride` — e.g. `swapDevices = lib.mkForce [ ... ];`, or disable the whole filesystem override with `virtualisation.fileSystems = lib.mkForce { };`.
- Since the VM's disk image (when using a bootloader) is built from the same extended config, options in `vmVariant` also apply to that image — usually what you want.
- On current releases (25.05/master) nesting `virtualisation.vmVariant` inside itself is not allowed (it throws).

So: one config, `virtualisation.vmVariant = { ... }`, and `nixos-rebuild build-vm` picks them up automatically.","confidence":0.9,"gaps":["Did not run an eval of `system.build.vm` / `virtualisation.vmVariant` (the eval tool was unavailable in this session); all claims verified by reading build-vm.nix and qemu-vm.nix on master, release-25.05, release-24.11 and release-23.11","Behavior on nixpkgs releases older than 23.11 (pre-`extendModules` form of the option) not checked"],"escalate_forced":"no"}

