{"answer":"Set `PermitRootLogin = "no"` in your `services.openssh.settings` block — that's a passthrough to `sshd_config`, and `"no"` forbids all root logins over SSH:

```nix
services.openssh = {
  enable = true;
  # openFirewall = true;  # if you need to expose port 22
  settings.PermitRootLogin = "no";
};
```

Details:

- `PermitRootLogin` accepts `yes`, `no`, `without-password` (a.k.a. `prohibit-password`). The OpenSSH default is `prohibit-password`, i.e. root can still log in by key. `"no"` closes that off entirely.
- `services.openssh.settings` is freeform, so any `sshd_config(5)` directive works there. The official NixOS wiki's recommended server baseline:

  ```nix
  services.openssh = {
    enable = true;
    openFirewall = true;
    settings = {
      PasswordAuthentication = false;
      KbdInteractiveAuthentication = false;
      PermitRootLogin = "no";
      AllowUsers = [ "myUser" ];
      MaxAuthTries = 3;
    };
  };
  ```

- If you add `AllowUsers`, keep yourself in the list, and re-check `nixos-rebuild` before locking yourself out — new SSH connections are refused as soon as the service restarts (existing sessions survive until closed).

Verified against the official NixOS wiki's SSH page (2026-07), whose example uses exactly `PermitRootLogin = "no"` for this purpose.","confidence":0.9,"gaps":["Did not read the nixpkgs openssh module source directly (the file no longer sits at nixos/modules/services/networking/openssh.nix and the GitHub API was rate-limited/403), so the stated default (prohibit-password) is from OpenSSH defaults + the wiki, not a source read at a pinned rev"],"escalate_forced":"no"}

