{"answer":"**Short version: use `den.lib.policy.route` in new code.** `den.batteries.forward` is the older battery that `route` is built from; it's kept for backwards compatibility and for a couple of extras (per-item `each`, adapter modules).

## What each one is

- **`policy.route`** (used as `den.lib.policy.route` inside a policy) — a policy effect that declares one *delivery edge*: take the content collected from class `fromClass` and place it into class `intoClass` at attrpath `path` (or `intoPath`). `reinstantiate = true` keeps the module verbatim (needed when the target re-instantiates it, e.g. a `user` system inside `nixos`); optional `guard` / `adaptArgs`. In the source it's documented as *"Tier 1 delivery — replaces den.batteries.forward for the common case"*, implemented as permanent sugar over the lower-level `deliver` primitive (`nix/lib/policy-effects.nix`).

- **`den.batteries.forward`** — the legacy battery (`nix/lib/forward.nix`: `forwardEach`/`forwardItem`). Same core job, but it packs more options into one call: `each` (fan out over a list of items), `fromClass`/`intoClass`/`intoPath` as per-item functions, `fromAspect`, `fromCtx`, `guard`, `adaptArgs`, `adapterModule`, `evalConfig`, `mapModule`, and it falls back to `den.classes.<class>.forwardTo` when `intoClass`/`intoPath` are omitted.

## The difference

Both move a custom class's content into another class. The maintainer docs put it exactly this way (`docs/.../explanation/choosing-a-mechanism.mdx`):

> *"`forward` is the older battery with more options in one call; `policy.route` is the policy effect it is built from. Den's own `os`, `user` and `wsl` classes use `route`. Reach for `route` in new code and for `forward` when you need one of its extras (per-item `each`, adapter modules) in a single expression."*

And in the current source, `forward`'s machinery is consumed *by* the policy pipeline (`nix/lib/aspects/fx/edges/route.nix` handles the legacy `__complexForward` specs as synthesize edges). So `route` is the forward primitive, and `forward` is the ergonomic wrapper around it. In discussion #582 the maintainers said the same thing: "batteries.forward is implemented with policies now, and is mostly provided for backwards compatibility" — the battery only *looks* more powerful because its complexity is decomposed across the policy tools, which compose better.

## Which to choose

- **New code: `policy.route`.** Den's own `os`, `user` and `wsl` classes use it; `homeManager`, `hjem` and `maid` use `forward`. Example straight from the docs:

  ```nix
  { den, ... }: {
    den.classes.files = { };
    den.policies.files-to-flake-parts = _: [
      (den.lib.policy.route {
        fromClass = "files";
        intoClass = "flake-parts";
        path = [ "files" ];
      })
    ];
    den.schema.flake-parts.includes = [ den.policies.files-to-flake-parts ];
  }
  ```

  (note: the route spec takes `path`, not `intoPath` — `intoPath` is the forward API's name, kept as a back-compat alias).

- **Reach for `den.batteries.forward`** when you're defining a new custom class that must fan out per item in one expression — the classic case is "one forward rule per user in `host.users`" (`each = lib.attrValues host.users`, `intoPath = user: [ "users" "users" user.userName ]`) or when you need an `adapterModule` for a custom submodule type. That's the canonical "Custom Nix Classes" example, and `forward` remains fully supported for it.

## How I know

Checked in `denful/den` (`main`): `nix/lib/policy-effects.nix` (`route`/`deliver` docs), `nix/lib/forward.nix` (the battery), `nix/lib/aspects/fx/edges/route.nix` (route/complex-forward materialization), `docs/src/content/docs/explanation/choosing-a-mechanism.mdx` and `docs/src/content/docs/guides/custom-classes.mdx`, and discussion #582 (May 31 2026) where the maintainers answer this exact question.","confidence":0.9,"gaps":["Source was read over the web (raw.githubusercontent.com) at denful/den main — clone was refused in this thread, so no exact commit SHA is pinned for the cited files","'batteries.forward is implemented with policies' is corroborated by route.nix consuming __complexForward specs via handlers/forward.nix's buildForwardAspect, but I did not read handlers/forward.nix in full"],"escalate_forced":"no"}

