{"answer":"**Short version:** in den, the user↔host association is entity-level data — no aspect or policy can add a user to a host. The built-in `host-to-users` policy only walks the users a host has already declared, and aspect content lands in class modules (`nixos`/`darwin`/`homeManager`/`user`/…), never merged back into the entity definition. So the *list of names* stays at the entity layer — but den gives you two sanctioned ways to do exactly what you're after.

**1. If your "host profile" is a host kind — `den.schema.host` decides the users**

Each host instance is a gen-schema instance built on top of the `den.schema.host` module (`nix/lib/entities/host.nix`: `mkInstanceType { extraModules = … } den.schema.host`), and `users` is an ordinary mergeable option there (`attrsOf userType`, default `{}`). So a module applied to all hosts of the kind can carry the shared user list:

```nix
# one place, for every host of the kind
den.schema.host = {
  config.users = {
    alice = { };
    bob = { };
  };
};
```

Every host gets those users; per-host `den.hosts.<system>.<name>.users` entries merge on top, so per-host additions/overrides stay local. The schema reference page (`docs/src/content/docs/reference/schema.mdx`) recommends exactly this shape for repetitive per-host user metadata — its example centralizes `users.<name>.classes` via `den.schema.user`. This is the only den mechanism where a profile *literally* decides which users a host gets.

**2. If the profile must be an aspect (shared by a subset of hosts) — names as stubs, all content in the aspect**

Keep each host's declaration down to names only:

```nix
den.hosts.x86_64-linux.igloo = {
  users = { alice = { }; };   # name stub — one line per host
  includes = [ den.aspects.desktop-profile ];
};
```

and let the profile aspect carry everything user-relevant. Cross-entity routing is built into the pipeline (no battery needed):

```nix
den.aspects.desktop-profile = {
  includes = [ den.batteries.define-user ];   # account creation: users.users.<name> + home dir
  provides.to-users = {
    user = { user, ... }: { extraGroups = [ "wheel" ]; };  # → users.users.<name> per user
    homeManager = { /* … */ };                        # → every user's home (host decides)
  };
};
```

Variants:

- `den.batteries.host-aspects` on the *user* side (`den.aspects.alice.includes = [ den.batteries.host-aspects ]`) flips the decision: each user opts into the host's whole home tree.
- A `{ user, ... }:` aspect in the host's includes runs once per user and can emit `nixos.users.users.${user.userName}…` for per-user conditions (e.g. `user.name == "alice"`).

**What policies can't do:** the policy effect set (`resolve`/`include`/`exclude`/`deliver`/`route`/`provide`/`instantiate`/`spawn`/`pipe`) has no effect that creates user entities — `host-to-users` is the only user fan-out and it reads `host.users`. Policies can only *gate* (`den.schema.host.includes` activates for every host of the kind; `den.lib.policy.when`/`den.lib.policy.for` narrow by context), so conditional profiles work, user creation doesn't.

**How I know:** `modules/policies/core.nix` — `den.policies.host-to-users` fans out over `lib.attrValues host.users` and is in `den.schema.host.includes`; `nix/lib/entities/host.nix` — the `users` option and the instance built on `den.schema.host`; `modules/aspects/batteries/define-user.nix` — `{ host, user }` → `nixos.users.users.${user.userName} = { name, home, isNormalUser = true; }`; `modules/aspects/batteries/host-aspects.nix` — policy spawn projecting the host aspect tree for `user.classes`; the Mutual Providers and Schema guide pages for the `provides.to-users` / schema-base-module patterns.
