{"answer":"`attribute 'den' missing` means some attrset you're indexing — almost certainly `inputs` or `config` — has no `den` in it. In a fresh den setup that's one of two missing pieces:

**1. You never declared the `den` flake input.**
Add it to your `flake.nix` inputs:
```nix
inputs.den.url = "github:denful/den";
```
Any reference to `inputs.den` (e.g. `imports = [ inputs.den.flakeModule ]`) throws exactly `attribute 'den' missing` if this line is absent.

**2. Your module set never imports den, so `config.den` (and the `den` module arg) don't exist.**
den's `den.*` options are declared by `modules/options.nix` and its `den` module argument is set by `nix/nixModule/default.nix` (`_module.args.den = config.den`). Both are pulled into your pipeline only if one of your modules imports den:
```nix
# modules/den.nix
{ inputs, den, ... }:
{
  imports = [ inputs.den.flakeModule ];   # ← this is the line people miss
  den.hosts.x86_64-linux.igloo = { ... };
  den.aspects.igloo = { ... };
};
```
`inputs.den.flakeModule` is just a module that recursively imports all of den's `modules/*.nix` (via `nix/flakeModule.nix` → `modules/lib.nix` → den-lib's nixModule). You do **not** need flake-parts for it — the official minimal template says so in a comment. If you're on flake-parts, the den guide adds one more: include `inputs.den.flakeOutputs.flake` in your module list.

The working shape, from den's official `templates/minimal`:
```nix
# flake.nix
{
  inputs = {
    nixpkgs.url = "https://channels.nixos.org/nixos-unstable/nixexprs.tar.xz";
    import-tree.url = "github:vic/import-tree";
    den.url = "github:denful/den";
  };
  outputs =
    inputs:
    (inputs.nixpkgs.lib.evalModules {
      modules = [ (inputs.import-tree ./modules) ];
      specialArgs = { inherit inputs; };
    }).config.flake;
}
```

