{"answer":"Yes — den has a built-in **`os` class** for exactly this: "a convenience class for settings that should be forwarded into both `nixos` and `darwin` classes" (`modules/aspects/batteries/os-class.nix`). You write the shared settings once under `os`, and den routes them into the right class per host:

```nix
# modules/shared-base.nix
{
  den.aspects.shared-base = {
    # identical settings, written once:
    os = { pkgs, ... }: {
      environment.systemPackages = [ pkgs.git ];
      programs.git.enable = true;
      # ...
    };
    # static option sets work too: os.environment.variables.FOO = "bar";
  };
}

# each host just includes it
den.aspects.igloo.includes = [ den.aspects.shared-base ];   # x86_64-linux  → lands in nixos
den.aspects.apple.includes = [ den.aspects.shared-base ];   # aarch64-darwin → lands in darwin
```

How it works: the battery registers the class and a built-in policy `den.policies.os-to-host` that emits `den.lib.policy.route { fromClass = "os"; intoClass = host.class; path = [ ]; }`, gated on `host.class` being `nixos` or `darwin` — so every host's `os` content merges at the root of its own OS config. It's on by default: `inputs.den.flakeModule` (`nix/flakeModule.nix`) imports every file under `modules/`, batteries included, so nothing to set up. Den's own CI pins this behavior: `templates/ci/modules/public-api/os-class.nix` and `os-class-host.nix` test `den.aspects.shared-base = { os.networking.hostName = "shared"; }` included by both an NixOS and a Darwin host and assert it lands in both configs, including the module-function form (`os = { lib, ... }: { ... }`).

Caveats:

- The shared set must be **valid in both module systems**. NixOS and nix-darwin share the nixpkgs-derived options (`programs.*`, `environment.*`, many `services.*`), but NixOS-only options (e.g. `boot.kernelPackages`) will fail the darwin host's eval with "option does not exist" — and vice versa.
- Values that genuinely differ per OS stay under the per-class keys: `nixos.system.stateVersion = "25.05"` / `darwin.system.stateVersion = 6` alongside `os` (the `den.default` example in `templates/example/modules/aspects/defaults.nix` does exactly this mixing).
- `os` only routes to the two OS root classes — home-manager content still goes under its own `homeManager` key in the same aspect.
- Fallback if you're not using the standard `flakeModule` wiring: define the module function once (`shared = import ./shared.nix;`) and point both class keys at it (`den.aspects.x = { nixos = shared; darwin = shared; }`) — plain Nix sharing, which den's per-class wrapping accepts as-is.

