{"answer":"It evaluates to **`1`** — the `let` binding wins, and the `a = 2` in the `with` set is never consulted for that reference.

**Why.** Nix is statically scoped, and `with` is not a lexical scoping construct:

- A `let` binding is an *explicit* (lexical) definition. The `a` in the body is bound to it at parse time, so resolving `a` is just an environment-slot lookup — it never looks at any `with` set.
- A `with` set provides only an *implicit* (fallback) lookup: it is consulted **only for names that are not lexically bound** in an enclosing scope. Since `a` is lexically bound by the `let`, the `with`'s `a = 2` is shadowed and ignored.

The Nix manual states the rule directly (`doc/manual/source/language/scope.md`): an explicit definition (from `let`, `rec`, or a function literal) can replace a definition of any type, but "an implicit definition can only replace another implicit definition" — and implicit definitions are created only by `with`. So `let` beats `with` no matter which one is written first: `with { a = 1; }; let a = 2; in a` is `2` as well.

**Source check** (NixOS/nix, `master`, `src/libexpr/eval.cc`):

- `ExprWith::eval` builds an environment with exactly **one** slot — the set itself:
  ```cpp
  Env & env2(state.mem.allocEnv(1));
  env2.up = &env;
  env2.values[0] = attrs->maybeThunk(state, env);
  body->eval(state, env2, v);
  ```
  The set's attributes are not placed into lexical slots.

- `EvalState::lookupVar` resolves a lexically bound variable (`var.fromWith == null`, which is our `a`) directly from the environment chain, without touching the `with` set:
  ```cpp
  if (!var.fromWith)
      return env->values[var.displ];
  ...
  if (auto j = env->values[0]->attrs()->get(var.name))
      return j->value;   // only reached for names not lexically bound
  ```

Sanity check of the converse: with no `let` in scope, `with { a = 2; }; a` would indeed be `2` (that's the fallback path above).

Practical note: this lexical/shadowing asymmetry is exactly why `with` is considered a footgun and is discouraged in Nixpkgs-style code.","confidence":0.95,"gaps":["Did not execute the expression in a live Nix evaluator: the eval tool evaluates flake attributes of a repo, and there is no repo at a rev containing this expression; the result is verified from the evaluator source and manual on master instead","clone was unavailable in this thread, so source was fetched as raw files from github.com (NixOS/nix master) rather than read from a checked-out tree"],"escalate_forced":"no"}

{"answer":"**Why it recurses:** In the NixOS module system, `config` is not "your module's config" — it is the *final merged configuration*, i.e. the very value the system is computing from all modules' `config` attributes, including yours. It's a fixed point: `config = f(config)`.

Concretely, when the system merges modules it forces your module's `config` right away — `mergeModules` in `lib/modules.nix` calls `pushDownProperties m.config` on every module. Your top-level `if config.foo.enable then ...` therefore evaluates the condition *at collection time*, before any option value exists. If `foo.enable` is defined in your own module's `config` (the classic case: it's set inside the `then`-branch), computing its final value means merging its definitions — which includes your module's `config`, i.e. the very `if` expression being evaluated. Cycle → Nix reports "infinite recursion". (The system even ships a hint for this: the `config` argument passed to modules is wrapped in an `addErrorContext` saying "if you get an infinite recursion here, you probably reference `config`… consider importing unconditionally, and using `mkEnableOption` and `mkIf`" — `lib/modules.nix`, `doCollect`.)

**The fix — the standard nixpkgs pattern:** declare the option under `options`, and gate the config with `lib.mkIf`:

```nix
{ config, lib, ... }:
let
  cfg = config.foo;
in
{
  options.foo = {
    enable = lib.mkEnableOption "foo";  # declares the option, default = false
    # ...other options...
  };

  config = lib.mkIf cfg.enable {
    # evaluated only when foo is enabled
  };
}
```

Why this works: `lib.mkIf cond content` is just `{ _type = "if"; condition = cond; content = content; }` — the condition is *stored, not evaluated*. `pushDownProperties` wraps your config attributes with these lazy wrappers without forcing the condition, and the condition is only evaluated in `dischargeProperties`, i.e. when a specific option's value is actually forced. The source comment on `pushDownProperties` states it plainly: "This transform is the critical step that allows `mkIf` conditions to refer to the full configuration without creating an infinite recursion." This is exactly how every enable-gated module in nixpkgs is written — e.g. `nixos/modules/programs/gnupg.nix`: `config = mkIf cfg.agent.enable { ... }` while `agent.enable` is only *declared* (`mkOption { type = types.bool; default = false; }`) in the same file.

**One caveat that will bite you:** `mkIf cfg.enable` is safe because `cfg.enable`'s value is computable *without* the definitions it gates (it has a declaration default and/or is set by the user elsewhere). If your module defines `foo.enable` *inside* the gated branch (self-reference), you get infinite recursion even with `mkIf`. In that case define the flag unconditionally — e.g. `config.foo.enable = lib.mkDefault true;` outside the `mkIf` — and gate only the dependent options.

Rule of thumb: declare under `options`, gate with `lib.mkIf` referencing `config.<opt>`, and avoid bare top-level `if config....` in module bodies. (And if your goal was conditional *imports*: `imports` can't depend on `config` at all — import unconditionally and gate with `mkIf`/`mkEnableOption`.)

Sources: NixOS/nixpkgs (current master, read via raw.githubusercontent on 2025-07-04): `lib/modules.nix` (`mergeModules`, `pushDownProperties`, `dischargeProperties`, `mkIf`, `doCollect`) and `nixos/modules/programs/gnupg.nix`.","confidence":0.8,"gaps":["No live eval: the asker gave no repo/rev, so the recursion was not reproduced with the eval tool; mechanism verified by reading nixpkgs master source via web (clone was unavailable in this environment)","Assumed foo.enable is defined in the module's own config (the classic self-referential case); the exact cycle shape depends on the asker's actual module content, which was not provided"],"escalate_forced":"no"}

