{"answer":"There are two supported mechanisms, and both work the same from the module's point of view: you name your value, and any module function that destructures it by that name receives it.

## Option A — `specialArgs` (the `evalModules` argument)

```nix
lib.evalModules {
  specialArgs = {
    myValue = "hello";
  };
  modules = [
    { myValue, ... }: {
      config.greeting = myValue;
    }
  ];
}
```

Every attribute of `specialArgs` is merged into the argument set each module is called with, so `myValue` is available as a plain function argument to every module (including ones reached via `imports`).

## Option B — the `config._module.args` option (preferred for everything not needed in `imports`)

```nix
lib.evalModules {
  modules = [
    {
      config._module.args = {
        myValue = "hello";
      };
    }
    { myValue, ... }: {
      config.greeting = myValue;
    }
  ];
}
```

## How I know

Read from `NixOS/nixpkgs`, `lib/modules.nix` (master):

- `doCollect` passes `{ inherit lib options specialArgs; _class = ...; _prefix = ...; config = ... } // specialArgs` to module collection — i.e. `specialArgs` attrs are part of the module arguments.
- `applyModuleArgs` resolves each declared argument of a function module as `args.${name} or config._module.args.${name}`, with an error context "noting that argument `${name}` is not externally provided, so querying `_module.args` instead" — this is exactly why Option B works.
- The `_module.args` option is typed `types.lazyAttrsOf types.raw` and documented as "Additional arguments passed to each module in addition to ones like `lib`, `config`, and `pkgs`, `modulesPath`. This option is also available to all submodules."
- The old top-level `args` argument of `evalModules` is deprecated in favor of `config._module.args` (warning text in the same file).
- Real-world usage: `nixos/lib/eval-config.nix` defines `config._module.args = { noUserModules; baseModules; extraModules; modules; }` so any NixOS module can destructure those as arguments.

## Which one to pick

- Use `specialArgs` **only** when the value must exist during import resolution, i.e. you reference it inside `imports = [ ... ]` (imports are computed before `config` is evaluated, so `_module.args` is not available there). The `specialArgs` doc comment in `modules.nix` says exactly this: use it for arguments needed when resolving module structure, and `_module.args` for everything else.
- Otherwise prefer `_module.args`: it's lazy (values aren't forced just because the option exists) and keeps `specialArgs` small — the header comment in `modules.nix` warns that large `specialArgs` make module sets harder to move into submodules.

Caveats:
- Submodules do **not** inherit args from their parent module (exception: the `name` argument); `_module.args`/`specialArgs` do propagate to them.
- `lib`, `config`, `options` and `specialArgs` are always provided and cannot be overridden via `_module.args`.
- If a module destructures a name that is in neither `specialArgs` nor `_module.args`, evaluation fails with the "not externally provided, so querying `_module.args`" attribute error — a useful signal that you forgot to register the value.","confidence":0.9,"gaps":["No live eval run: verified from nixpkgs master source (lib/modules.nix, nixos/lib/eval-config.nix) rather than evaluating a snippet; clone was unavailable in this environment, files fetched as raw"],"escalate_forced":"no"}

