{"answer":"Set `acceptTerms` on the ACME module — that is the assertion `enableACME = true` trips when it's the only ACME-related option you've set:

```nix
security.acme.acceptTerms = true;   # nixpkgs master / 26.x
```

On nixpkgs ≤ 25.05 the same option lives at `services.acme.acceptTerms = true;` (the module was moved to `security.acme` on master).

The exact assertion, from the ACME module source:

> "You must accept the CA's terms of service before using the ACME module by setting `security.acme.acceptTerms` to `true`. For Let's Encrypt's ToS see https://letsencrypt.org/repository/"

**Why nothing else is required.** The nginx module wires the rest up for you (verified in `nginx/default.nix` on master): for every vhost with `enableACME` it auto-creates `security.acme.certs."example.org"` with `reloadServices = [ "nginx.service" ]`, `group = "nginx"`, `extraDomainNames = serverAliases`, and a webroot taken from the vhost's `acmeRoot` — which defaults to `/var/lib/acme/acme-challenge` (`vhost-options.nix`), with `dnsProvider` forced to null. So the module's other ACME assertion ("exactly one of `dnsProvider` / `webroot` / `listenHTTP` / `s3Bucket` is required") is already satisfied by the defaults, and nginx gets the `.well-known/acme-challenge/` location plus the systemd wiring (nginx `Wants/After` the `acme-*` services, `nginx-config-reload`) automatically.

Minimal working config:

```nix
security.acme.acceptTerms = true;

services.nginx = {
  enable = true;
  virtualHosts."example.org" = {
    forceSSL = true;     # keep the port-80 listener; it answers the HTTP-01 challenge
    enableACME = true;
  };
};
```

If the message you saw was different, the ACME-related assertions in the source are:

- *"…enableACME requires a HTTP listener to answer to ACME requests"* — your vhost has no plain-HTTP (port 80) listener (e.g. `onlySSL = true`); keep an HTTP listen.
- *"Exactly one of the options `security.acme.certs.<name>.dnsProvider`, `.webroot`, `.listenHTTP` and `.s3Bucket` is required"* — happens if you set the vhost's `acmeRoot = null` and didn't configure the cert yourself; set one of those (or `acmeRoot`) on the cert/vhost.
- *"…enableACME and …useACMEHost are mutually exclusive"* — set only one of them.

Note: I verified this against nixpkgs master source only; I couldn't fetch the ≤ 25.05 release-branch module from here (raw GitHub 404'd for non-master refs), and I didn't evaluate your config (no repo/rev was provided), so if the assertion text you saw matches none of the four above, paste it and I'll check against your exact rev.","confidence":0.85,"gaps":["nixpkgs release branches (≤25.05) not fetchable from this tooling; the `services.acme.acceptTerms` option name there is from prior knowledge, not source-verified","asker's nixpkgs version and the exact assertion text are unverified; config was not evaluated (no repo/rev provided)"],"escalate_forced":"no"}

