{"answer":"The mechanism is a **NixOS specialisation**: a variant defined in your system configuration, built with it, and given its own boot entry. I verified all of this against nixpkgs (master) source.

## 1. Define it in your config

`nixos/modules/system/activation/specialisation.nix` defines `specialisation` as `attrsOf` of a submodule with `inheritParentConfig` (default `true`) and `configuration`:

```nix
{
  # base system: e.g. amdgpu (open source)
  hardware.graphics.enable = true;

  # variant for the same host: NVIDIA proprietary driver
  specialisation.nvidia = {
    # inheritParentConfig = true is the default: the whole base
    # configuration is included; override only what differs
    configuration = {
      hardware.nvidia.enable = true;
      hardware.nvidia.modesetting.enable = true;
      services.xserver.videoDrivers = [ "nvidia" ];

      # (GRUB only) prettier name for the boot menu
      boot.loader.grub.configurationName = "NixOS (nvidia)";
    };
  };
}
```

Each `specialisation.<name>.configuration` is evaluated as a full NixOS system layered over your base config (set `inheritParentConfig = false` for a completely independent system). The module computes `children = mapAttrs (name: cfg: cfg.configuration.system.build.toplevel) config.specialisation`, i.e. each specialisation is its own toplevel derivation, and the base system's builder symlinks them into `$out/specialisation/<name>` (via `system.systemBuilderCommands` in that file).

## 2. Build it with your normal rebuild

`nixos-rebuild switch` (or `boot`) builds the base system **and all specialisations**, because they are build inputs of the toplevel through those symlinks. No special flag is needed to make them appear at boot.

## 3. What you get at the boot menu

- **GRUB**: `install-grub.pl` (`nixos/modules/system/boot/loader/grub/install-grub.pl`) scans `$path/specialisation/*` for the current system. The current generation becomes a submenu containing `NixOS - Default` plus one `menuentry` per specialisation, named `NixOS - <name>` where the default `<name>` is `<specialisation-name> - <date> - <version>`, overridable via `boot.loader.grub.configurationName` (grub.nix writes `$out/configuration-name`).
- **systemd-boot**: specialisations get their own entries directly after their parent generation, inheriting the parent's sort key (documented on `boot.loader.systemd-boot.sortKey`).

There's also a runtime switch without rebooting: `sudo /run/current-system/specialisation/nvidia/bin/switch-to-configuration test` (from the option's doc).

## Caveats

- Specialisation boot entries are only listed for the **current** generation (the GRUB builder doesn't enumerate specialisations of older generations).
- Specialisation names must not contain `/`; nested specialisations are ignored.
- This works for the GPU driver because a specialisation is a complete system closure — the nvidia stack is in *its* kernel/system while the base keeps amdgpu. The option's doc uses exactly this shape of use case ("boot the same configuration as you use, but with another kernel").

## How I know

Read in nixpkgs master: `nixos/modules/system/activation/specialisation.nix` (option definition, children, `$out/specialisation/` symlinks, runtime-switch command), `nixos/modules/system/boot/loader/grub/grub.nix` + `install-grub.pl` (submenu generation and naming), `nixos/modules/system/boot/loader/systemd-boot/systemd-boot.nix` (sortKey docs), `nixos/modules/installer/tools/tools.nix` (`nixos-rebuild` is now the Python `nixos-rebuild-ng`; `nixos-version` lists `lib.attrNames config.specialisation`).","confidence":0.85,"gaps":["Did not verify whether current nixos-rebuild-ng (Python) still has a --specialisation <name> flag for building a single specialisation in isolation; the old bash nixos-rebuild had it, and the nixos-rebuild-ng source was not fetchable (404 on every path tried, GitHub API rate-limited)","Did not evaluate the asker's actual configuration (no repo/rev given); the GPU-driver example is the standard pattern, not checked against their file","systemd-boot entry title details taken from the sortKey option docs, not from systemd-boot-builder.py itself"],"escalate_forced":"no"}

