Put the VM-only options in `virtualisation.vmVariant` in your configuration:

```nix
# /etc/nixos/configuration.nix (or any module of it)
{
  # regular options — used by normal rebuilds and also in the VM
  networking.hostName = "foo";
  services.openssh.enable = true;

  # added only to the evaluation that builds the VM
  virtualisation.vmVariant = {
    networking.firewall.enable = false;  # e.g. reachable over the default SLiRP user net
    virtualisation.memorySize = 2048;
    virtualisation.cores = 2;
    virtualisation.graphics = true;
  };
}
```

**Why this works** (checked in NixOS/nixpkgs, `master`):

- `nixos-rebuild build-vm` builds `config.system.build.vm` (`nixos/default.nix` exposes `inherit (eval.config.system.build) vm vmWithBootLoader;`).
- `nixos/modules/virtualisation/build-vm.nix` defines that as
  `system.build.vm = lib.mkDefault config.virtualisation.vmVariant.system.build.vm;`, where `vmVariant = extendModules { modules = [ ./qemu-vm.nix ]; }` — i.e. the VM is built from a re-evaluation of your full config plus the QEMU-VM module plus your `vmVariant` module. Options under `vmVariant` only exist in that VM-variant evaluation, so they don't affect a normal `switch`/`build` on real hardware.
- `vmVariant` is declared with the full NixOS config type (`inherit (vmVariant) type`), so it takes any module: an attrset of options or a `{ config, ... }: { ... }` function. There is a sibling, `virtualisation.vmVariantWithBootLoader`, for `nixos-rebuild build-vm-with-bootloader`.

**Caveats**

- VM runner options (`virtualisation.memorySize`, `.cores`, `.graphics`, `.qemu.*`, …) are only declared inside the VM variant (by `qemu-vm.nix`), so set them under `vmVariant`; setting them in the base config errors as unknown options on a normal rebuild.
- The low-level primitive is `lib.mkVMOverride` — `mkVMOverride = mkOverride 10` in `lib/modules.nix`, documented as "Labels a definition with used by `nixos-rebuild build-vm`". NixOS uses it inside `qemu-vm.nix` to force VM-appropriate values (e.g. `fileSystems` from `virtualisation.fileSystems`, empty `swapDevices`/initrd LUKS devices, `modesetting` video driver) above normal values — even above `mkForce`. If *you* use it in a regular config it applies as a super-strong `mkForce` to every evaluation, so for user config prefer `virtualisation.vmVariant`.
- For flakes, `nixos-rebuild --flake build-vm` / `nix build .#nixosConfigurations.<name>.config.system.build.vm` build the same `system.build.vm` derivation, so the same `vmVariant` mechanism applies.

