{"answer":"Set it in your NixOS configuration:

```nix
{
  services.openssh = {
    enable = true;
    settings.PermitRootLogin = "no";
  };
}
```

`services.openssh.settings` maps directly to `sshd_config(5)` keys, so `PermitRootLogin = "no"` lands verbatim in the generated `/etc/ssh/sshd_config` and forbids root logins entirely, including key-based ones.

Valid values (from the option's type in the module source): `"yes"`, `"without-password"`, `"prohibit-password"`, `"forced-commands-only"`, `"no"`. Note the NixOS default is `"prohibit-password"` — password logins as root are already blocked, but key-based root logins are still allowed. If root SSH access works on your box today, that's the default at work; `"no"` closes it fully.

Practical notes:
- Make sure at least one non-root user has an SSH key (`users.users.<name>.openssh.authorizedKeys.keys`) before switching, so you don't lock yourself out on a server.
- The `sshd` unit restarts automatically when the config file changes (the module sets `restartTriggers`), so `nixos-rebuild switch` is all you need — no manual service restart.

How I verified: I read the NixOS module source (`nixos/modules/services/networking/ssh/sshd.nix`, NixOS/nixpkgs master), where `PermitRootLogin` is declared with `default = "prohibit-password"` and the enum type above, and `settings` is rendered into `sshd_config` by the module's settings generator.","confidence":0.9,"gaps":["Did not run eval against the asker's own configuration (no repo/rev provided); the module source was read from NixOS/nixpkgs master"],"escalate_forced":"no"}

